## HOW TO WRITE THE SUBJECT

Use this sequence once, then write the body. Do not generate candidate subjects and compare them. The subject is finished. Do not look for a better one. Do not reconsider it; select the first valid subject and continue.

1. Choose the first matching kind:

| The diff mainly... | kind |
|---|---|
| changes only repo maintenance, tooling, config, or dependencies | chore |
| changes only CI/workflow files | ci |
| changes only build scripts or packaging | build |
| changes only documentation or comments | docs |
| changes only tests | test |
| changes only formatting, with no behavior change | style |
| fixes wrong behavior | fix |
| adds behavior that did not exist | feat |
| restructures code with no behavior change | refactor |
| changes code to make it faster | perf |

2. Use the directory or subsystem that owns most of the diff as the scope. Otherwise omit the scope and the parentheses entirely.
3. Write one lowercase imperative clause describing behavior. There is no length limit; stop at its end.
4. Confirm `<kind>(<scope>)?!?: <description>`: a scope uses lowercase letters, digits, `/`, `_`, or `-` only — no dots, no uppercase; `!` marks a breaking change; one space follows the colon; the description starts with a lowercase letter or digit; and the frontmatter value is unquoted. Quotes become part of the subject and are rejected.

## HOW TO WRITE THE BODY

For any change beyond a one-line typo, formatting, or comment change, include one `## Body` item explaining why it was needed and what it affects. Use `## Body Summary`, `## Body Details`, and `## Body Footer` for migration steps, breaking changes, or issue references.

## WORKED EXAMPLES

- Bad: `chore: update files` → `feat(mcp): add structured commit retries`, with a body explaining what structured retries solve.
- Bad: `fix: stuff` → `fix(parser): preserve prefixed transcript lines`, with a body explaining the edge case that dropped them.
- Bad: `refactor: rename files` → `refactor(auth): extract token validation to dedicated module`, with a body explaining what callers must do differently.

